1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,583
In both cases a shared branch on the server
now points at a history that isn't the

2
00:00:09,623 --> 00:00:14,581
one everybody had. A branch is a named
line of commits, the saved snapshots of a

3
00:00:14,621 --> 00:00:19,642
project. A force push overwrites the
server's branch, so commits can drop off it.

4
00:00:19,682 --> 00:00:24,390
And "not an ancestor" means that the
branch's history no longer contains the

5
00:00:24,430 --> 00:00:29,638
deployed commit. In both cases the obvious
repair is another forced push, to put the

6
00:00:29,678 --> 00:00:30,700
old history back.

7
00:00:31,040 --> 00:00:33,948
That repair is itself a
rewrite of a shared branch.

8
00:00:33,988 --> 00:00:38,456
Done without two checks, it becomes the
second incident: it removes whatever a

9
00:00:38,496 --> 00:00:41,693
colleague has legitimately
pushed or built on top since.

10
00:00:41,733 --> 00:00:46,490
Today you learn the two checks, and what
the restoring command looks like when it's

11
00:00:46,530 --> 00:00:48,224
done properly. Guess them now.

12
00:00:48,564 --> 00:00:54,134
A first question, before any cause is named.
By default, Git on the server keeps no

13
00:00:54,174 --> 00:00:59,609
record of where a branch used to point.
So who, or what, could still know the old

14
00:00:59,649 --> 00:01:02,178
position? Name two. Say them out loud.

15
00:01:07,018 --> 00:01:11,777
A teammate's clone that hasn't fetched
since. And a clone that has fetched: its

16
00:01:11,817 --> 00:01:16,455
reflog, Git's local record of where a
ref has been, keeps the previous value.

17
00:01:16,495 --> 00:01:19,615
A clone is each person's
own copy of the repository.

18
00:01:21,455 --> 00:01:25,379
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

19
00:01:25,419 --> 00:01:30,774
This is a debrief of Incident 2,
incidents/02-force-push-wrong-branch, and Incident

20
00:01:30,814 --> 00:01:36,169
4, incidents/04-production-history-rewritten.
You must have generated and attempted

21
00:01:36,209 --> 00:01:39,873
both, with a command log and
a severity rating. Have you?

22
00:01:39,913 --> 00:01:42,213
If you haven't, stop the video here.

23
00:01:44,553 --> 00:01:46,756
The causes are named in the next section.

24
00:01:47,096 --> 00:01:51,659
These are the first drills in which
the damaged state is on the server.

25
00:01:51,699 --> 00:01:56,715
That changes where the evidence is. From
video 185: a bare repository keeps no

26
00:01:56,755 --> 00:02:01,966
reflog, so the history of a server-side
branch exists only as the sum of what the

27
00:02:02,006 --> 00:02:05,338
clones remember, plus whatever
the platform records.

28
00:02:05,378 --> 00:02:10,200
From video 42 you know what a forced
push destroys and what a lease checks.

29
00:02:10,240 --> 00:02:14,738
From video 131 you know the rule that
would have refused these pushes.

30
00:02:15,078 --> 00:02:20,421
Both incidents are SEV 2 on the scale
of video 185, and the second one is the

31
00:02:20,461 --> 00:02:24,824
textbook's example of rating by
the window: it shipped nothing.

32
00:02:26,664 --> 00:02:28,077
After this video you can:

33
00:02:28,417 --> 00:02:32,328
Find out what a forced push replaced
and who still has the old commits.

34
00:02:32,668 --> 00:02:37,076
Check two things before restoring: what
has been built on the new tip, and who has

35
00:02:37,116 --> 00:02:37,673
fetched it.

36
00:02:38,013 --> 00:02:41,898
Restore the branch without losing a
legitimate commit made after the rewrite.

37
00:02:42,238 --> 00:02:43,935
Realign teammates' clones.

38
00:02:44,275 --> 00:02:47,364
Walk from the root cause to
controls on several layers.

39
00:02:49,204 --> 00:02:54,153
Witnesses. When a server branch has been
replaced, the old value is known to three

40
00:02:54,193 --> 00:02:59,264
kinds of witness. A clone that hasn't fetched
since: its remote-tracking branch, the

41
00:02:59,304 --> 00:03:03,219
clone's record of the server's
branch, still holds the old value.

42
00:03:03,259 --> 00:03:07,844
A clone that has fetched: the reflog of
its remote-tracking branch holds the

43
00:03:07,884 --> 00:03:11,068
previous value as the entry
before the forced update.

44
00:03:11,108 --> 00:03:15,997
And anything that recorded the commit by
ID: a tag, which is a fixed name for one

45
00:03:16,037 --> 00:03:17,883
commit, or a deployment record.

46
00:03:18,223 --> 00:03:23,314
git status isn't a witness of the present.
It reports on origin/main, your clone's

47
00:03:23,354 --> 00:03:26,130
memory. git ls-remote
origin asks the server.

48
00:03:26,470 --> 00:03:31,831
Incident 2: the root cause. The feature
branch was created from origin/main and so

49
00:03:31,871 --> 00:03:35,915
has origin/main as its upstream,
the server branch it follows.

50
00:03:35,955 --> 00:03:41,448
With push.default=upstream, a bare git push
updates the upstream branch whatever its

51
00:03:41,488 --> 00:03:46,981
name. --force removed the fast-forward check,
the rule that a push may only add to a

52
00:03:47,021 --> 00:03:50,406
branch. The server accepted
a forced update of main.

53
00:03:50,446 --> 00:03:55,940
The default, simple, would have refused.
Layers: Git configuration in one clone, and

54
00:03:55,980 --> 00:03:57,389
a missing GitHub rule.

55
00:03:57,729 --> 00:04:03,163
You met the first half of this in video 181:
a branch created from a remote-tracking

56
00:04:03,203 --> 00:04:08,572
branch follows it. There it made a pull
useless. Here, with one configuration value

57
00:04:08,612 --> 00:04:12,287
and one flag, it sent a feature
branch's commits to main.

58
00:04:12,627 --> 00:04:18,003
Try it now. Thirty seconds, read-only. In
the lab shell, or in a repository of your

59
00:04:18,043 --> 00:04:21,592
own with a recent Git, run
git config get push.default.

60
00:04:21,632 --> 00:04:22,832
Say what it prints.

61
00:04:25,172 --> 00:04:29,572
If it prints nothing, the setting is
unset and Git uses the default, simple.

62
00:04:29,612 --> 00:04:34,012
If it prints upstream, you've found the
first half of Incident 2 on your own

63
00:04:34,052 --> 00:04:34,480
machine.

64
00:04:34,820 --> 00:04:39,859
Incident 4: the root cause. A branch that
deployment records refer to by commit ID

65
00:04:39,899 --> 00:04:45,001
accepted a forced update. An interactive
rebase, which rewrites a series of commits

66
00:04:45,041 --> 00:04:48,160
from an editable list, was
meant to "tidy" history.

67
00:04:48,200 --> 00:04:52,558
It created new commits and lost one.
--force replaced the server's ref.

68
00:04:52,598 --> 00:04:57,638
Then "reset to the server", given as advice
to a confused colleague, made a second

69
00:04:57,678 --> 00:05:01,850
clone adopt the rewrite, and a
legitimate commit was shipped on top.

70
00:05:01,890 --> 00:05:04,266
Layers: Git, and a missing GitHub rule.

71
00:05:04,606 --> 00:05:09,400
"Same code, fewer commits" is a claim about
trees. A tree is the complete snapshot

72
00:05:09,440 --> 00:05:14,292
of files that a commit records. So the claim
can be tested: compare the tree of the

73
00:05:14,332 --> 00:05:16,473
old tip with the tree of the new one.

74
00:05:16,813 --> 00:05:21,017
Who did it. In rewritten history, read
the committer and the reflogs, not the

75
00:05:21,057 --> 00:05:25,151
author. The author wrote the change,
and the committer created this commit.

76
00:05:25,191 --> 00:05:29,561
A folded commit keeps its original author
and names the person who rewrote it as

77
00:05:29,601 --> 00:05:30,112
committer.

78
00:05:30,452 --> 00:05:35,043
The two checks before restoring. Here are
the two checks promised at the start.

79
00:05:35,083 --> 00:05:39,967
First: what has been built on the new tip?
If a legitimate commit sits on top of the

80
00:05:40,007 --> 00:05:43,016
rewritten history, the
restore has to carry it over.

81
00:05:43,056 --> 00:05:47,764
Second: who has fetched the new tip? Every
clone that adopted it will be diverged

82
00:05:47,804 --> 00:05:52,572
after the restore and has to be realigned,
with a proof that nothing of its own is

83
00:05:52,612 --> 00:05:52,865
lost.

84
00:05:53,205 --> 00:05:57,768
And one check on your own evidence: is
your copy of the old value the newest good

85
00:05:57,808 --> 00:06:01,348
one? Another clone's unfetched
value must be an ancestor of it.

86
00:06:01,688 --> 00:06:06,729
The restoring command. A forced push with
an explicit lease, the value you expect

87
00:06:06,769 --> 00:06:11,685
the server's branch to have. It names the
server's current, bad value: git push

88
00:06:11,725 --> 00:06:15,951
--force-with-lease=<branch>:<current-bad-id>
origin <good>:<branch>.

89
00:06:15,991 --> 00:06:21,157
If anyone pushed meanwhile, it's refused.
Before it, an anchor for the state that's

90
00:06:21,197 --> 00:06:26,301
about to be replaced, and, where the removed
commits are somebody's work, a branch

91
00:06:26,341 --> 00:06:28,371
of their own on the server first.

92
00:06:28,711 --> 00:06:33,385
When a forced restore is the wrong fix.
Had someone already built on the bad tip,

93
00:06:33,425 --> 00:06:37,982
the right move in Incident 2 would be git
revert of the bad commits, which adds

94
00:06:38,022 --> 00:06:42,114
commits that undo them. In Incident
4 the textbook compares two routes.

95
00:06:42,454 --> 00:06:47,326
One: keep the rewritten history and re-add
the fix, with no further force, but then

96
00:06:47,366 --> 00:06:52,297
the deployed tag is never an ancestor again
and every recorded ID points off-branch.

97
00:06:52,337 --> 00:06:56,144
Two: restore the old history and
carry the one newer commit over.

98
00:06:56,184 --> 00:06:59,399
For a branch whose IDs are
recorded elsewhere, restore.

99
00:06:59,739 --> 00:07:04,789
GitHub, not Git: the rule and the instruments.
A ruleset is GitHub's named list of

100
00:07:04,829 --> 00:07:09,878
rules for chosen branches. "Block force
pushes" in a ruleset on the default branch

101
00:07:09,918 --> 00:07:13,602
rejects this push. It's enabled
by default in a new ruleset.

102
00:07:13,642 --> 00:07:18,381
For a production branch the textbook's
control is a ruleset that blocks force

103
00:07:18,421 --> 00:07:23,533
pushes, restricts deletions, requires a
pull request, and has an empty bypass list.

104
00:07:23,873 --> 00:07:28,237
When no clone has the old value,
GitHub offers instruments of its own.

105
00:07:28,277 --> 00:07:33,333
The repository's Activity view lists force
pushes with the user and a comparison.

106
00:07:33,373 --> 00:07:38,555
A PushEvent in the Events API carries the
before ID, within the last 300 events and

107
00:07:38,595 --> 00:07:42,518
30 days. And the references API
can create a branch at that ID.

108
00:07:45,358 --> 00:07:50,031
GitHub documents each instrument and not
this combined procedure, publishes no

109
00:07:50,071 --> 00:07:55,046
retention period for commits that no ref
reaches, and documents no "restore" action

110
00:07:55,086 --> 00:07:59,577
inside the Activity view. GitHub's own
advice is the plain Git route: ask a

111
00:07:59,617 --> 00:08:02,538
collaborator who still has
the commit to push it.

112
00:08:04,378 --> 00:08:09,254
Think of the server's branch as a signpost
at a junction, and of every clone as a

113
00:08:09,294 --> 00:08:13,260
traveller who photographed the
signpost the last time they passed.

114
00:08:13,300 --> 00:08:18,358
Somebody turned the signpost. There's no
camera at the junction: the server keeps no

115
00:08:18,398 --> 00:08:23,214
record of where the sign used to point.
But every traveller's last photograph is

116
00:08:23,254 --> 00:08:27,219
dated, and some travellers have
two photographs, before and after.

117
00:08:27,559 --> 00:08:30,402
So the investigation is a
collection of photographs.

118
00:08:30,442 --> 00:08:34,893
And the repair, turning the sign back, has
to consider the travellers who already

119
00:08:34,933 --> 00:08:39,550
followed the new direction: one of them has
built something at the end of that road.

120
00:08:39,890 --> 00:08:44,383
Where the picture breaks: on GitHub there's
a camera after all, the Activity view

121
00:08:44,423 --> 00:08:48,749
and the events, with limits and lifetimes
of its own, and with the caveats you

122
00:08:48,789 --> 00:08:51,491
heard. In the lab's bare
repository there's none.

123
00:08:51,831 --> 00:08:56,323
The model for the restoring push: say out
loud what you believe the server holds,

124
00:08:56,363 --> 00:09:00,239
and let the server refuse if you're
wrong. That sentence is the lease.

125
00:09:02,079 --> 00:09:06,947
Before the demo, what we know so far is
two reports: main was force-pushed, and a

126
00:09:06,987 --> 00:09:12,037
release job refuses to deploy. The picture
of the server's branch, before and after,

127
00:09:12,077 --> 00:09:14,522
with its witnesses, is drawn in the demo.

128
00:09:16,362 --> 00:09:21,391
From here on the screen shows the solutions.
Is your own command log beside you?

129
00:09:23,731 --> 00:09:25,043
Then compare as we go.

130
00:09:25,383 --> 00:09:26,967
Observe. 🟢 SAFE.

131
00:09:27,307 --> 00:09:30,705
Our status says up to date,
with ca03e42 as the tip.

132
00:09:30,745 --> 00:09:34,673
The server says b4554be, a commit
this clone has never seen.

133
00:09:34,713 --> 00:09:38,971
The status is a statement about
the last contact with the server.

134
00:09:39,311 --> 00:09:44,548
Fetch. A fetch moves remote-tracking refs.
Here the old value is evidence, and it's

135
00:09:44,588 --> 00:09:49,698
safe: this clone's own main still holds
it, and the fetch records it in a reflog.

136
00:09:50,038 --> 00:09:55,126
A plus sign and "forced update". Ahead
2, behind 2, without having committed

137
00:09:55,166 --> 00:10:00,254
anything. And the reflog of origin/main:
the entry before the forced update,

138
00:10:00,294 --> 00:10:03,426
origin/main@{1}, is the
value before the event.

139
00:10:03,766 --> 00:10:06,354
What changed, and is my
old value the newest?

140
00:10:06,694 --> 00:10:11,335
The server lost two reviewed commits
and gained two commits marked WIP.

141
00:10:11,375 --> 00:10:16,873
Then the check on our own evidence: Ravi
hasn't fetched, his origin/main is 27215c8,

142
00:10:16,913 --> 00:10:21,620
and that's an ancestor of our old
value. So ours is the newest good one.

143
00:10:21,960 --> 00:10:27,913
The cause. Look at the clone that pushed.
Quick quiz: what will the brackets of her

144
00:10:27,953 --> 00:10:32,318
feature branch say? A,
origin/feature/dedupe. B, origin/main.

145
00:10:32,358 --> 00:10:34,557
C, nothing at all. Your answer?

146
00:10:39,397 --> 00:10:44,528
B. feature/dedupe follows origin/main.
push.default is upstream, set in this clone.

147
00:10:44,568 --> 00:10:49,575
And her remote-tracking reflog records the
push. She pushed "only my branch", and

148
00:10:49,615 --> 00:10:53,189
with this configuration her
branch's destination was main.

149
00:10:53,529 --> 00:10:58,577
Preserve. 🟢 SAFE for the anchor. The second
command is additive: a new branch on the

150
00:10:58,617 --> 00:10:59,001
server.

151
00:11:03,541 --> 00:11:07,395
Asha's two commits existed on the
server only as the tip of main.

152
00:11:07,435 --> 00:11:12,308
Now they have their own branch, so restoring
main removes nothing from the server.

153
00:11:12,648 --> 00:11:16,656
Restore. 🔴 DANGEROUS: a forced
update of main. The five answers.

154
00:11:16,696 --> 00:11:20,199
What it changes: the server's
main, back to the old tip.

155
00:11:20,239 --> 00:11:24,247
What it can destroy: anything
pushed to main since the bad push.

156
00:11:24,287 --> 00:11:28,168
Preview: git ls-remote origin
once more, and the lease itself.

157
00:11:28,208 --> 00:11:32,280
Recovery: the commits being removed
now live on their own branch.

158
00:11:32,320 --> 00:11:37,150
When appropriate: here, because the
commits being removed are unreviewed, are

159
00:11:37,190 --> 00:11:39,427
preserved, and nobody built on them.

160
00:11:43,967 --> 00:11:48,207
The lease says: I believe the
server's main is b4554be.

161
00:11:48,247 --> 00:11:49,530
Refuse otherwise.

162
00:11:49,870 --> 00:11:51,488
Fix the cause, in her clone.

163
00:11:51,828 --> 00:11:56,131
The upstream of her feature branch now
is the branch of the same name, and

164
00:11:56,171 --> 00:11:58,243
push.default is back to the default.

165
00:11:58,583 --> 00:11:59,234
Verify.

166
00:11:59,574 --> 00:12:03,776
The server holds main at ca03e42
and feature/dedupe at b4554be.

167
00:12:03,816 --> 00:12:08,960
The check passes: every reviewed commit
on main, no WIP commit, and the cause

168
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:10,913
removed in the pushing clone.

169
00:12:14,253 --> 00:12:17,569
The messages. At once:
nobody pulls or pushes main.

170
00:12:17,609 --> 00:12:22,900
After the restore: git fetch, then git
status -sb. A clone whose main shows ahead

171
00:12:22,940 --> 00:12:28,296
stops and asks. To the CTO: main pointed
at unreviewed work for a stated number of

172
00:12:28,336 --> 00:12:31,587
minutes, nothing was lost,
the rule goes on today.

173
00:12:31,927 --> 00:12:32,502
Observe.

174
00:12:40,042 --> 00:12:44,096
Our untouched production is ahead
4, behind 2. We made no commits.

175
00:12:44,136 --> 00:12:48,687
"Diverged, and I made no commits" means
the server's history was replaced.

176
00:12:49,027 --> 00:12:52,223
Witnesses. Three independent
records of the old tip.

177
00:12:52,563 --> 00:12:58,721
Three times 3277739. The ancestry test
exits 1: that's the release job's refusal.

178
00:12:58,761 --> 00:13:04,919
And the last line shows who did what: the
folded commit keeps its original author

179
00:13:04,959 --> 00:13:07,061
and names Ravi as committer.

180
00:13:07,401 --> 00:13:11,992
Three records name the same old tip: our
untouched branch, the entry before the

181
00:13:12,032 --> 00:13:14,219
forced update, and the deployment tag.

182
00:13:14,559 --> 00:13:19,372
git range-diff finds no pairs: four old
commits on one side, two new ones on the

183
00:13:19,412 --> 00:13:19,736
other.

184
00:13:20,076 --> 00:13:25,137
Test the claim. "Same code, fewer commits."
Predict the output of a tree comparison

185
00:13:25,177 --> 00:13:27,596
if the claim were true. Say it out loud.

186
00:13:32,436 --> 00:13:35,856
Were the claim true, only
Asha's new file would differ.

187
00:13:35,896 --> 00:13:41,077
Two files differ. One is Asha's new commit.
The other is billing/tax.py, which lost

188
00:13:41,117 --> 00:13:46,299
its rounding. The interactive rebase dropped
the commit "Round tax to two decimals"

189
00:13:46,339 --> 00:13:51,521
when a line of the todo list was deleted.
That's the finance ticket about unrounded

190
00:13:51,561 --> 00:13:52,276
tax amounts.

191
00:13:52,616 --> 00:13:52,976
How.

192
00:13:53,304 --> 00:13:58,675
Ravi's reflogs: a rebase started four
commits back, two fixups, a finish, and an

193
00:13:58,715 --> 00:13:59,081
amend.

194
00:13:59,421 --> 00:14:03,776
Restore. First the anchor, then the
legitimate commit, then the push.

195
00:14:03,816 --> 00:14:08,937
🟢 for the anchor. 🟡 for the cherry-pick,
which copies the commit onto the current

196
00:14:08,977 --> 00:14:14,160
branch. 🔴 for the forced push, conditional
on the server's current value, 4fae70f.

197
00:14:14,200 --> 00:14:19,384
It's appropriate because this branch's IDs
are recorded elsewhere, the state being

198
00:14:19,424 --> 00:14:24,098
replaced is anchored, and the one
legitimate commit has been carried over.

199
00:14:32,438 --> 00:14:37,318
Asha's commit is now a323df1, on top of
the real history, and the rounding commit

200
00:14:37,358 --> 00:14:42,237
1d8b2fc is in the list again. One commit
on the rewritten line wasn't part of the

201
00:14:42,277 --> 00:14:44,302
mistake, and the restore keeps it.

202
00:14:44,642 --> 00:14:50,086
Realign. Each clone that adopted the rewrite
proves that nothing of its own is lost

203
00:14:50,126 --> 00:14:53,059
before it moves. 🟡
CAUTION: git reset --keep.

204
00:14:53,399 --> 00:14:58,565
In Asha's clone, git cherry -v marks her
own commit with a minus sign: the server

205
00:14:58,605 --> 00:15:03,771
has an equivalent change. The only plus
sign is the folded commit, which is being

206
00:15:03,811 --> 00:15:07,756
retired on purpose. In Ravi's
clone the same single plus sign.

207
00:15:07,796 --> 00:15:09,556
Then each moves with --keep.

208
00:15:09,896 --> 00:15:10,547
Verify.

209
00:15:13,887 --> 00:15:17,405
The release job's own test exits
0. The tax function rounds.

210
00:15:17,445 --> 00:15:22,386
The difference between the rewritten state
and the restored one is exactly one file,

211
00:15:22,426 --> 00:15:27,189
the one that was wrong. The check passes
for the server and for all three clones.

212
00:15:27,529 --> 00:15:33,027
The messages. To the team: the new tip,
and the two commands, git cherry -v, then

213
00:15:33,067 --> 00:15:38,155
git reset --keep, with the rule for any
unexpected plus sign: stop and ask.

214
00:15:38,195 --> 00:15:43,831
To the CTO: nothing bad was deployed because
the release job refused, a dropped fix

215
00:15:43,871 --> 00:15:47,044
was caught, and the missing
rule goes on today.

216
00:15:47,384 --> 00:15:52,870
From root cause to controls on several
layers. A client default: push.default left

217
00:15:52,910 --> 00:15:56,845
at simple. A habit: --force-with-lease,
never bare --force.

218
00:15:56,885 --> 00:16:00,753
A platform rule: block force
pushes on the default branch.

219
00:16:00,793 --> 00:16:06,414
For production, also restrict deletions,
require a pull request, and keep the bypass

220
00:16:06,454 --> 00:16:11,804
list empty. A pipeline check: the ancestry
test in the release job, which is the

221
00:16:11,844 --> 00:16:17,330
control that worked. And a line in the
handbook: "diverged, and I made no commits"

222
00:16:17,370 --> 00:16:20,968
means the server's history
was replaced. Stop and ask.

223
00:16:22,808 --> 00:16:24,456
Five mistakes to watch for.

224
00:16:24,796 --> 00:16:28,614
Trusting git status about the server.
Root cause: it compares with the

225
00:16:28,654 --> 00:16:32,581
remote-tracking branch, which is the
clone's memory of the last contact.

226
00:16:32,921 --> 00:16:37,685
Restoring with a bare --force. Root cause:
nothing then checks whether somebody

227
00:16:37,725 --> 00:16:42,792
pushed to the branch in the meantime, so
the recovery can remove a colleague's work.

228
00:16:43,132 --> 00:16:46,328
Restoring without looking at
what was built on the new tip.

229
00:16:46,368 --> 00:16:50,825
Root cause: a legitimate commit made after
the rewrite sits on the history that is

230
00:16:50,865 --> 00:16:51,977
about to be replaced.

231
00:16:52,317 --> 00:16:55,987
Advising "reset to the server"
to someone who sees "diverged".

232
00:16:56,027 --> 00:17:00,534
Root cause: with no local commits,
divergence means the server's history was

233
00:17:00,574 --> 00:17:04,304
replaced, and the reset makes a
second clone adopt the rewrite.

234
00:17:04,644 --> 00:17:08,478
Accepting "same code, fewer
commits" without a tree comparison.

235
00:17:08,518 --> 00:17:13,396
Root cause: an interactive rebase can drop
a commit with one deleted line of the

236
00:17:13,436 --> 00:17:15,733
todo list, and only the trees show it.

237
00:17:17,573 --> 00:17:22,072
Now, out of the lab. A billing team
deploys from a branch named production.

238
00:17:22,112 --> 00:17:26,731
Its release job has one safety check
that nobody remembers adding: the commit

239
00:17:26,771 --> 00:17:30,786
currently running must be an ancestor
of the commit to be deployed.

240
00:17:30,826 --> 00:17:32,481
One morning the job refuses.

241
00:17:32,821 --> 00:17:37,596
The engineer on call writes one sentence
in the channel, asks two colleagues not to

242
00:17:37,636 --> 00:17:42,121
fetch or reset, and collects three
witnesses of the old tip: her own untouched

243
00:17:42,161 --> 00:17:46,357
branch, the reflog of her remote-tracking
branch, and the deployment tag.

244
00:17:46,397 --> 00:17:51,056
They agree. She compares trees, finds that
a tax-rounding fix is missing from the

245
00:17:51,096 --> 00:17:55,466
rewritten history, and connects it to
a finance ticket filed the day before.

246
00:17:55,806 --> 00:18:00,512
She finds one legitimate commit on top of
the rewrite, anchors the rewritten state,

247
00:18:00,552 --> 00:18:05,259
carries that commit over, and restores with
a lease that names the server's current

248
00:18:05,299 --> 00:18:05,602
value.

249
00:18:05,942 --> 00:18:10,794
Her summary rates the incident SEV 2 although
nothing was deployed, because the only

250
00:18:10,834 --> 00:18:14,813
thing between the rewritten branch
and production was that one check.

251
00:18:14,853 --> 00:18:19,531
The postmortem has three lines the team
remembers. The control that worked was an

252
00:18:19,571 --> 00:18:23,725
automated ancestry check. The control
that was missing was one checkbox.

253
00:18:23,765 --> 00:18:27,918
And the advice "reset to the server"
turned one affected clone into two.

254
00:18:29,758 --> 00:18:35,911
Your turn. Do Lab 37.1, "A force push
to the wrong branch (incident 2)", in

255
00:18:35,951 --> 00:18:42,270
lab-manual/m37-incident-drills-platform.md,
from a freshly generated sandbox.

256
00:18:42,610 --> 00:18:46,001
Before you fetch, write down what
you'll record first and why.

257
00:18:46,041 --> 00:18:50,538
Before the restore, write the two checks
and their results, the ID your lease will

258
00:18:50,578 --> 00:18:54,854
name, and what you'll do if the lease is
refused. After the restore, write the

259
00:18:54,894 --> 00:18:58,229
message to the team with the
exact commands for their clones.

260
00:18:58,269 --> 00:18:59,668
Type the recovery by hand.

261
00:19:00,008 --> 00:19:05,826
The challenge is Lab 37.2, "Production branch
history is rewritten (incident 4)", in

262
00:19:05,866 --> 00:19:06,803
the same file.

263
00:19:10,643 --> 00:19:15,455
Answer out loud. A strong answer first says
where the old value comes from, naming

264
00:19:15,495 --> 00:19:20,070
at least two kinds of witness, and how
you make sure it's the newest good one.

265
00:19:20,110 --> 00:19:24,982
It then gives the two checks as questions
about other people's work: one about what

266
00:19:25,022 --> 00:19:28,355
sits on top of the new tip,
one about who has adopted it.

267
00:19:28,695 --> 00:19:33,185
It describes the restoring command by its
parts: what is pushed, to which ref, with

268
00:19:33,225 --> 00:19:37,770
which condition, and what is anchored
beforehand. And it names the case in which you

269
00:19:37,810 --> 00:19:40,664
wouldn't force at all, and
what you would do instead.

270
00:19:40,704 --> 00:19:44,375
Mention the platform rule that would
have refused the original push.

271
00:19:46,215 --> 00:19:47,143
Let's land this.

272
00:19:47,483 --> 00:19:52,520
A server keeps no reflog; the old value of
a replaced branch comes from clones that

273
00:19:52,560 --> 00:19:57,658
have not fetched, from remote-tracking
reflogs, and from tags or deployment records.

274
00:19:57,998 --> 00:20:01,844
Before restoring, check what was built
on the new tip and who has fetched it.

275
00:20:02,184 --> 00:20:06,859
The restoring push carries an explicit
lease naming the server's current value,

276
00:20:06,899 --> 00:20:11,217
after the state being replaced has
been anchored or given its own branch.

277
00:20:11,557 --> 00:20:16,601
Clones that adopted the rewrite prove with
git cherry -v that nothing of their own

278
00:20:16,641 --> 00:20:19,329
is missing, then move
with git reset --keep.

279
00:20:19,669 --> 00:20:24,383
Controls sit on several layers: a client
default, a habit, a ruleset, and an

280
00:20:24,423 --> 00:20:26,322
ancestry check in the pipeline.

281
00:20:28,162 --> 00:20:35,648
Read sections 30.6 and 30.8. Then generate
Incident 6, incidents/06-pr-500-changes,

282
00:20:35,688 --> 00:20:39,003
and attempt it before the next video.

283
00:20:39,343 --> 00:20:44,300
You can now put a replaced branch back
without losing what a colleague built since,

284
00:20:44,340 --> 00:20:49,357
and say which control was missing on which
layer. Practise the first restore by hand

285
00:20:49,397 --> 00:20:54,414
before you go on. Next time, a new report:
"I pushed one commit and the pull request

286
00:20:54,454 --> 00:20:58,628
shows 500 files". Until then, look
at the state first and type second.

287
00:20:58,668 --> 00:21:00,072
See you in the next one.
